{"abstract":"Several researchers have been studying how working with materials such as yarn can shift how we think and theorize relations to the body and others, by bringing together feminist values at the intersection of data, technology, and hands-on making. Extending such prior work, our research, anchored in craft-based knowledge production, aims to contribute with explorations on knotting as a feminist approach to data materialization. We present a year-long process consisting of 4 workshops we conducted with participants, in which we used differ-ent forms of knotting to materialize data about bodies being part of our academic institution and the Covid-19 pandemic. Presenting the workshops and their outcomes, we discuss how knotting as an approach to materializing data can: 1. put a focus on missing data, 2. surface corporeal and affective vulnerabilities, 3. contribute to making new relations with other (non-human) bodies, and 4. trouble notions of time.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Tsaknaki, Vasiliki","Tatjana Reime, Lara","Cohn, Marisa","Pérez-Bustos, Tania"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-754067"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","OECD"],"country":["Colombia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National University of Colombia"],"keyword":["Body","Crafts","Data","Feminism","Materialization","khipu","knotting","macramé"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"knotting data as a feminist approach to data materialization","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21606/drs.2024.879","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-754067","ids":["itu-242c5614-07a9-4745-a8ef-0a07a28ab52a"],"keyword":["body","crafts","data","feminism","khipu","knotting","macramé","materialization"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-5951EB1867AF2EEE8CE809DD532780C5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.designresearchsociety.org/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=3405&context=drs-conference-papers","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://dl.designresearchsociety.org/drs-conference-papers/drs2024/researchpapers/270/","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.21606/drs.2024.879","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design","IT University of Copenhagen, Digital Design, Design Research Section","IT University of Copenhagen, Human-Computer Interaction and Design, Interaction Design Research Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"co","key":"national university of colombia","loc":["Colombia","America, South","OECD"],"name":"National University of Colombia","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasiliki","key":"tsaknakipau","last":"Tsaknaki","locid":"805cae2a-3462-4130-88de-cc7d94aec0a5","name":"Tsaknaki, Vasiliki","orcid":"0000-0003-2235-6078","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lara","key":"reimepau","last":"Reime","locid":"0baaf9f8-3126-4cb1-b22f-b9fceabb8b54","name":"Tatjana Reime, Lara","name_org":"Reime, Lara","orcid":"0000-0002-7475-2147","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Tania","key":"pérezbustospau","last":"Pérez-Bustos","name":"Pérez-Bustos, Tania","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.21606/drs.2024.879","status":"p","title":"DRS2024"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-242c5614-07a9-4745-a8ef-0a07a28ab52a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Knotting data as a feminist approach to data materialization","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-754067","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Digital methods, taken up in the collection and analysis of data, raise concerns around extraction, representation, care, consent, and participation familiar to feminist methodologies. At a feminist STS lab, specialising in digital methods, we convened some workshops to support research design in ongoing projects situated in the Lab’s community, with the aim to articulate feminist principles for digital methods. These projects, working with methods including digital ethnography, database implementation, and machine learning design raise feminist questions around centring care in data collection and participation, navigating hesitancies around extracting data from vulnerable subjects, and working through representational politics of data. These workshops, rather than congealing a set of feminist principles, generated a proliferation of disconcertments and troubles. We offer workshopping troubles as a way to navigate and theorise tensions in designing digital methods research. We account for how these workshops served as a method to elicit troubles, surfacing them for further analysis, and helped us shift from binding dichotomies to troubles that open ongoing inquiry. We reflect on how digital methods return us to troubles that, while well understood in feminist approaches to data, require articulation in practice and research design.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Perriam, Jessamy","Cohn, Marisa","Hockenhull, Michael","Tatjana Reime, Lara","Landa, Luis","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Friis, Henriette"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-730171"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Disconcertment","Ethnography","Feminist digital methods","Research Design","Science and Technology Studies","workshopping"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"workshopping troubles towards feminist digital methods","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08164649.2024.2321576","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-730171","identifier":[{"id":"85186618045","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-63137823-9862-4f33-a0d3-440a4269c108"],"keyword":["Feminist digital methods","disconcertment","ethnography","research design","science and technology studies","workshopping"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-70A0FC14E3B5B57117B14FBB1F3E6C98"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/08164649.2024.2321576","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jessamy","key":"perriampau","last":"Perriam","locid":"4ca57c3b-1d1f-4ecf-87b0-fe690510c887","name":"Perriam, Jessamy","orcid":"0000-0002-6362-8634","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lara","key":"reimepau","last":"Reime","locid":"0baaf9f8-3126-4cb1-b22f-b9fceabb8b54","name":"Tatjana Reime, Lara","name_org":"Reime, Lara","orcid":"0000-0002-7475-2147","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Luis","key":"landapau","last":"Landa","name":"Landa, Luis","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henriette","key":"friispau","last":"Friis","locid":"140f881b-1540-455b-8066-f0bc9ac6d82c","name":"Friis, Henriette","orcid":"0009-0008-1098-6674","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"]},"sid":["itu-63137823-9862-4f33-a0d3-440a4269c108"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Workshopping Troubles: Towards Feminist Digital Methods","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-730171","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Whilst STS has long studied lab work, the past few years have also seen the introduction of labs as social formations for doing STS enquiry. But what kind of contributions to pedagogy, particularly in technical universities, can STS inspired university labs make? We respond to the need to more deeply understand how teaching may be practiced as a site of STS experimentation by describing the work of the ETHOS Lab, our critical feminist lab at the IT University of Copenhagen, as ‘enlivening’. Using three events from 2017–2020 as case studies, we identify both the use of space and shaping of time as integral to how we have sought to trouble knowledge production in our own environment, particularly by creating sites and situations where teaching and research cannot be separated. By showing how our Lab activities intervene in ways of knowing and doing within university hierarchies and cultures, we aim to contribute an analysis of the critical potential of STS lab work in technical environments, and recommendations from our situated evolving space of feminist praxis within an interdisciplinary IT University.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Cakici, Baki","Cohn, Marisa","Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Ojala, Mace","Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-789556"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Ruhr University Bochum"],"keyword":["Computer Science","Feminist pedagogy","LAB","Placemaking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"spaceships and poetry enlivening the lab as a site of feminist critical pedagogy","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.17351/ests2023.1915","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-789556","ids":["itu-711e4444-5f30-43d1-96a7-2c354602ea71"],"keyword":["computer science","feminist pedagogy","lab","placemaking","timework"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C15951B8EA8B501240253FD3148DCFAF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.17351/ests2023.1915","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://estsjournal.org/index.php/ests/article/view/1915/815","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"ruhr university bochum","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Ruhr University Bochum","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mace","key":"ojalapau","last":"Ojala","name":"Ojala, Mace","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cæcilie Sloth","key":"laursenpau","last":"Laursen","locid":"3ef9211c-34b2-4061-ba59-c5800d6a5b32","name":"Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","orcid":"0000-0001-7102-7268","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.17351/ests2023.1915","issn":["24138053"],"issue":"1-2","journal":"Engaging Science, Technology, and Society","publisher":"Society for Social Studies of Science","status":"p","title":"Engaging Science, Technology, and Society","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-711e4444-5f30-43d1-96a7-2c354602ea71"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Spaceships and Poetry : Enlivening the Lab as a site of Feminist Critical Pedagogy","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-789556","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Through close analysis of fertility and menstrual cycle tracking apps, this contribution explores how reproductive bodies, and their temporalities are understood, made and reshaped with and through technologies. We rethink reproductive temporalities through the lens of Kafer’s (2013) and Forlano’s (2017) notion of queer/crip time to account for the different and individual temporalities of bodies. By tuning into the everyday rituals and temporalities that emerge when living with real-time data around sociocultural norms of reproduction (Forlano, 2017), we make visible how fertile bodies are in a constant state of becoming. Empirically, we home in on the processes of becoming or unbecoming fertile and engagements of self-tracking as well as practices of self-care by drawing on data inquired through digital ethnographic methods. To contextualize the analysis within broader, (sub-)cultural imaginaries of reproductive futures, we combine walkthroughs of apps (Light et al., 2018) with analysis of discussions and data-sharing practices on social network sites dedicated to fertility tracking and queer/non-binary experiences of reproduction.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Tatjana Reime, Lara","Cohn, Marisa","Tsaknaki, Vasiliki"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-718936"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital ethnography","Fertility tracking apps","Queer crip time","Reproductive temporalities","Self tracking"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fertile becoming reproductive temporalities with in tracking technologies","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102855001/pre-print_fertile_becoming_2023.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-718936","identifier":[{"id":"85198247032","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-fbd3ff6a-908d-4997-a8e8-45223b55cc14"],"keyword":["Digital ethnography","Fertility tracking apps","Queer/crip time","Reproductive temporalities","Self-tracking"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-AAABD1397F3CD55C3DB130BA255A8C0E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Andet","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/102855001/pre-print_fertile_becoming_2023.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, ETHOS Lab","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lara","key":"tatjanareimepau","last":"Tatjana Reime","locid":"0baaf9f8-3126-4cb1-b22f-b9fceabb8b54","name":"Tatjana Reime, Lara","orcid":"0000-0002-7475-2147","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasiliki","key":"tsaknakipau","last":"Tsaknaki","locid":"805cae2a-3462-4130-88de-cc7d94aec0a5","name":"Tsaknaki, Vasiliki","orcid":"0000-0003-2235-6078","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Lindsay","key":"balfourpedh","last":"Balfour","name":"Balfour, Lindsay","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9789819956050"],"pages":"73-97","publisher":"Palgrave Macmillan","status":"p","title":"FemTech: Intersectional Interventions in Women’s Digital Health"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85198247032"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-fbd3ff6a-908d-4997-a8e8-45223b55cc14"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Fertile Becoming: Reproductive Temporalities with/in Tracking Technologies","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-718936","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Menstruation and fertility tracking applications are of increasing concern in HCI research, as their use becomes more widespread. Methods are needed to understand how such applications become entangled in everyday practices. While these apps promise increased self-knowledge of reproductive potential by collecting intimate data about reproductive bodies, they also restrict the knowledge produced about users’ bodies and embed normative understandings of reproduction and gender. In this paper, we scrutinize the normativities of reproductive bodies by deploying the “walkthrough method” to uncover sociotechnical entanglements of the menstruation and fertility apps Clue, Tilly, and Drip. We discuss how the walkthrough method contributes to HCI's methodological repertoire for studying intimate bodily tracking apps and unpacking their normativities. We offer suggestions for using this method to critically analyze existing apps and extend approaches to design with and for a plurality of in/fertile bodies.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Tatjana Reime, Lara","Tsaknaki, Vasiliki","Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-677319"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","title":"ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Fertility apps","Human Computer Interaction HCI","Intimate data collection","Menstruation tracking","Normative understandings","Plurality in design","Reproductive bodies","Reproductive health","Sociotechnical entanglements","Walkthrough method"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"walking through normativities of reproductive bodies a method for critical analysis of tracking applications","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544548.3581450","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-677319","identifier":[{"id":"85160005909","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-716354a2-2bc8-4489-b1fa-4724ba37117b"],"keyword":["Fertility apps","Human-Computer Interaction (HCI)","Intimate data collection","Menstruation tracking","Normative understandings","Plurality in design","Reproductive bodies","Reproductive health","Sociotechnical entanglements","Walkthrough method"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-689E547FB404D5B32E97D56A9150B675"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3544548.3581450","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lara","key":"tatjanareimepau","last":"Tatjana Reime","locid":"0baaf9f8-3126-4cb1-b22f-b9fceabb8b54","name":"Tatjana Reime, Lara","orcid":"0000-0002-7475-2147","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vasiliki","key":"tsaknakipau","last":"Tsaknaki","locid":"805cae2a-3462-4130-88de-cc7d94aec0a5","name":"Tsaknaki, Vasiliki","orcid":"0000-0003-2235-6078","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3544548.3581450","eisbn":["9781450394215"],"pages":"1-15","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the 2023 CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85160005909"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-716354a2-2bc8-4489-b1fa-4724ba37117b"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Walking Through Normativities of Reproductive Bodies: A Method for Critical Analysis of Tracking Applications","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-677319","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"In this article, we suggest approaching writing as a vulnerable practice marked by an unstable boundary between bodies: bodies of text and bodies of writers. We present an exercise-method that we refer to as Monster Writing, which we have developed in order to engage with these instabilities as well as in order to address experiences of difficulty, anxiety and uncertainty in relation with the text and writing process. Though the writing process can at times be exciting and thrilling, and at other times perhaps a little tedious and mundane, for some it also presents (more than) occasional encounters with one's own insecurities, shame and doubt. We argue that this potentially more painful relationship between writer and text should be awarded more attention in scholarship on writing, and that a way of doing so is through the framework of feminist theory on vulnerability, embodiment, and the monstrous.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Blønd, Marie","Cohn, Marisa","Cakici, Baki","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Ferreira, Pedro","Feshak, Viktoriya","Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Sandbukt, Sunniva"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383081"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Technical University of Munich"],"keyword":["Feminist theory","Monster theory","Organizational studies","Vulnerability","Writing"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"writing bodies and bodies of text thinking vulnerability through monsters","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383081","identifier":[{"id":"85121653378","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"keyword":["feminist theory","monster theory","organizational studies","vulnerability","writing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5993E6636350380725B0F7E6033835DC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/gwao.12782","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"technical university of munich","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Technical University of Munich","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Viktoriya","key":"feshakpau","last":"Feshak","name":"Feshak, Viktoriya","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sunniva","key":"sandbuktpau","last":"Sandbukt","locid":"06d038a4-f659-4c53-8a6d-58e295ab603f","name":"Sandbukt, Sunniva","orcid":"0009-0002-4274-4991","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gwao.12782","issn":["09686673","14680432"],"issn_org":["09686673"],"journal":"Gender, Work and Organization","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Gender, Work and Organization"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121653378"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7b7818dc-cf90-442f-826c-fe4264251f64"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Writing bodies and bodies of text: Thinking vulnerability through monsters","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383081","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Pull requests facilitate inclusion and improvement of contributions in distributed software projects, especially in open source communities. An author makes a pull request to present a contribution as a candidate for inclusion in a code base. The request is inspected by maintainers and reviewers. The initiated process of review and collaborative improvement can be loaded with debates, opinions, and emotions. It heavily influences the atmosphere in the community. It can demotivate and detract contributors or it can fail to guard the code quality. Both problems put the existence of a community at risk. This mixed methods study aims to elucidate the mechanisms of evaluating pull requests in diverse open source software communities from the perspectives of developers and maintainers. We interviewed 30 participants from five different communities and conducted a survey with N=387 respondents. The data shows that acceptance of contributions in open source depends not only on technical criteria, but also significantly on social and strategic aspects. As a result, we identify three governance styles for pull requests: (1) protective, (2) equitable, and (3) lenient. While the protective style values trustworthiness and reliability of the contributor, the lenient style believes in creating a positive and welcoming environment where contributors are mentored to evolve contributions until the community standards are met. Each of the governance styles safeguards the quality of the project code in different ways. We hope that this material will help researchers and community managers to obtain a more nuanced view on the peculiarities of different communities and the strengths and weakness of their pull requests evaluation process.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Pardo, Raúl","Cohn, Marisa","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382826"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Decision making","Open source software","Pull Request","Pull Request Governance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pull request governance in open source communities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86330848/TSE_2021_preprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382826","identifier":[{"id":"85126671710","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-67d84e33-ffb9-4ebb-90d0-0a6cd9d2935a"],"keyword":["Decision Making","Open source software","Pull Request","Pull Request Governance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F6010B2F06E3A303FD421D8D5EE6F6FC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86330848/TSE_2021_preprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Raúl","key":"pardopau","last":"Pardo","locid":"d05daa00-c3d6-4a89-ba14-cfd1847fb0cc","name":"Pardo, Raúl","orcid":"0000-0003-0003-7295","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/tse.2021.3128356","issn":["00985589","19393520"],"issn_org":["00985589"],"issue":"12","journal":"IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering","pages":"4838-4856","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering","title_org":"I E E E Transactions on Software Engineering","volume":"48"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85126671710"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-67d84e33-ffb9-4ebb-90d0-0a6cd9d2935a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Pull Request Governance In Open Source Communities","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382826","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"When designing artificial intelligence (AI) in politicised contexts, such as the public sector, optimistic promises of what AI can achieve often shape decisions around which problems AI should address. Different epistemological views carry different understandings of what is considered the problem at hand, and, as we show in this paper, ethnographic perspectives often fail to match the politicised promises of AI. This paper reflects on personal experiences from an interdisciplinary research project that aimed to take a responsible approach to research and design AI for public services in Denmark. Seeking alternatives to the inflexible algorithms [3, 38] often used to automate or augment specific decision-making tasks in these contexts [1, 2, 35], our research project took a flexible approach to research and design and included ethnographic workplace studies to explore whether AI could both leverage the increasing powers of computing and retain the discretion of the user [23]. Following Mesman [33], we present three empirical moments that were particularly challenging for us as ethnographic researchers and influenced our project in important ways regarding the problems for AI to solve. Problematising [6] them, enabled us to surface how 'readiness', emerging from the politicised context of AI in Denmark, had confounded our efforts at interdisciplinary collaboration. Problematisation, then, allowed us to come to a new understanding of the problem at hand and open up a space to collaboratively re-imagine the problems for AI to solve. This paper is in the spirit of serving as a bridge between our initial and revised understanding, pointing to the ongoing discussion in HCI about 'bridging the gap' between ethnography and design. Our contribution is a discussion of how researchers and designers might engage with problematisation at the frontiers of HCI to develop an open-ended approach to collaborative AI design in politicised contexts.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Petersen, Anette C.M.","Cohn, Marisa Leavitt","Hildebrandt, Thomas T.","Møller, Naja Holten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-485905"],"event":{"end":"2021-07-13","place":"Virtual, Online, it","start":"2021-07-11","title":"14th Biannual Conference of the Italian SIGCHI Chapter: Frontiers of HCI, CHItaly 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Ethnography","Interdisciplinary research","Politics","Problematisation","Public services"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"thinking problematically as a resource for ai design in politicised contexts","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/281989572/_Thinking_Problematically_as_a_Resource_for_AI_Design_in.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-485905","identifier":[{"id":"85112707591","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-9ab0e7e6-ffe8-4b3d-a7f2-4e96491c4429"],"keyword":["AI design","Ethnography","Interdisciplinary research","Politics","Problematisation","Public digitisation","Public services"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F71E748B4749B92B824AE625C62584C4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/281989572/_Thinking_Problematically_as_a_Resource_for_AI_Design_in.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Computer Science, Software, Data, People & Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Anette C.M.","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","name":"Petersen, Anette C.M.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marisa Leavitt","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","name":"Cohn, Marisa Leavitt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas T.","key":"hildebrandtpau","last":"Hildebrandt","locid":"c1d69914-5f7a-4f0b-8ffb-766693004328","name":"Hildebrandt, Thomas T.","orcid":"0000-0002-7435-5563","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Naja Holten","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"1373f0c3-8420-4023-874a-25270bf667d6","name":"Møller, Naja Holten","orcid":"0000-0002-4324-3745","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3464385.3464738","eisbn":["9781450389778"],"pages":"1-8","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","series":"ACM International Conference Proceeding Series","status":"p","subtitle":"Proceedings of the 14th Biannual Conference of the Italian SIGCHI Chapter","title":"CHItaly 2021 - Frontiers of HCI"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112707591"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9ab0e7e6-ffe8-4b3d-a7f2-4e96491c4429"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"'Thinking problematically' as a resource for AI design in politicised contexts","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-485905","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper offers the related concepts of 'caring' and 'commoning', borrowed from feminist technoscience and feminist political economy, as both values for design and analytical lenses to scrutinize computer-cooperative infrastructures for activist interventions. The paper draws on the empirical work conducted in a European participatory design project confronting risks of social exclusion that result from precarious labor conditions increasingly affecting the European populations. We discuss how the combined concepts of caring and commoning present alternatives to capitalist accumulation, supporting activist and emancipatory aims by shedding light on dimensions related to work, affects, and politics.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Sciannamblo, Mariacristina","Cohn, Marisa Leavitt","Lyle, Peter","Teli, Maurizio"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-091467"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Rome La Sapienza"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"caring and commoning as cooperative work a case study in europe","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-091467","identifier":[{"id":"85118564835","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-8e8268f3-65c1-46a5-9af7-fb8c3615c816"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B247CADAB1E8E3308C23157E38C69826"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"university of rome la sapienza","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Rome La Sapienza","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Sustainability and Planning","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Sustainability and Planning, Danish Centre for Health Informatics","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Sustainability and Planning, Techno-Anthropology and Participation"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mariacristina","key":"sciannamblopau","last":"Sciannamblo","name":"Sciannamblo, Mariacristina","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa Leavitt","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","name":"Cohn, Marisa Leavitt","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"lylepau","last":"Lyle","name":"Lyle, Peter","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Maurizio","key":"telipau","last":"Teli","locid":"3fb25d29-be2b-4845-85fc-b192d8a5af29","name":"Teli, Maurizio","orcid":"0000-0002-6654-1788","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3449200","issn":["25730142"],"issue":"CSCW1","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","pages":"1-26","paperid":"126","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85118564835"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-8e8268f3-65c1-46a5-9af7-fb8c3615c816"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Caring and Commoning as Cooperative Work: A Case Study in Europe","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-091467","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper studies data work in an organizational context, and suggests speculative data work as a useful concept and the speculative dashboard as a design concept, to better understand and support cooperative work. Drawing on fieldwork in a Danish public sector organisation, the paper identifies and conceptualizes the speculative data work performed around processes of digitalization and the push to become data-driven. The speculative dashboard is proposed as a design concept and opportunity for design, using practices from speculative design and research to facilitate speculation about data?its sources, visualizations, practices and infrastructures. It does so by hacking the 'genre' of the business intelligence data dashboard, and using it as a framework for the juxtaposition of different kinds of data, facilitating and encouraging speculation on alternative visions for data types and use. The paper contributes an empirical study of organizational use of and attitudes towards data, informing a novel design method and concept for co-speculating on alternative visions of and for organizational data.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Hockenhull, Michael","Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383945"],"event":{"acronym":"CSCW","title":"ACM Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Business intelligence","Data visualization","Data work","Ethnography","Speculative design"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"speculative data work amp dashboards designing alternative data visions","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85672321/SDWD_pre_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383945","identifier":[{"id":"85175611527","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b7937331-5ab4-4ae6-810e-90c2cfc52cb4"],"keyword":["business intelligence","data visualization","data work","ethnography","speculative design"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CAC9F1047EDC8DD872A583782A18F25F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85672321/SDWD_pre_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3434173","issn":["25730142"],"issue":"CSCSW3","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","paperid":"264","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","title_org":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction - CSCW","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175611527"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b7937331-5ab4-4ae6-810e-90c2cfc52cb4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Speculative Data Work &amp; Dashboards : Designing Alternative Data Visions","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383945","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This article analyzes the role of hype in performing and translating corporate sociotechnical imaginaries of digital technologies, into the context of Danish society. Drawing on ethnographic fieldwork of technology events in Denmark, the paper proposes “hot air” as a concept to describe how hype for the future performs these imaginaries. The paper describes the overlapping sociotechnical imaginaries that dominate these events and the performative effects of hype and its critique, in articulating and translating them. The paper makes an empirical and conceptual contribution to the study of sociotechnical imaginaries, in particular their socio-material performance, the role of corporations in articulating them, and how hype is central to their translation.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Hockenhull, Michael","Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384413"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Big Tech","Corporations","Data","Digital transformation","Digitalization","Futures","Hot air","Hype","Sociotechnical imaginaries"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hot air amp corporate sociotechnical imaginaries performing and translating digital futures in the danish tech scene","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86073250/hockenhull_and_cohn_hot_air_and_corporate_sociotechnical_imaginaries_2020_accepted_manuscript_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384413","identifier":[{"id":"85101781619","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d5fb8f45-fae3-4ec3-98cd-e36fc92523e4"],"keyword":["Big Tech","Digitalization","corporations","data","digital transformation","futures","hot air","hype","sociotechnical imaginaries"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7F87E35516344687F840D98BC8E0376"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86073250/hockenhull_and_cohn_hot_air_and_corporate_sociotechnical_imaginaries_2020_accepted_manuscript_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/1461444820929319","issn":["14614448","14617315"],"issn_org":["14614448"],"issue":"2","journal":"New Media & Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"New Media & Society","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101781619"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d5fb8f45-fae3-4ec3-98cd-e36fc92523e4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hot air &amp; corporate sociotechnical imaginaries : Performing and translating digital futures in the Danish tech scene","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384413","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"On the eve of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) coming into effect we, a university laboratory, marked the occasion with an interactive installation called Compliance. Data traces from Compliance were subsequently processed by the lab, here enacted in the form of a play. While much discussion has centered around modern 'black-boxed' processing of data, less attention has been paid to the value of the data itself, and whether it merits use. We draw on dramaturgical methods for both analysis and presentation [15], allowing for readers to imagine staging their own, different, versions of the event. Drawing on the ambiguous ontological status of (yet unexamined) data, we offer a discussion on the value of data, its use and non-use, as well as how to live with this ambivalence, continuously negotiating social contracts about our further conduct with the data.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Ferreira, Pedro","Cohn, Marisa","Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Hockenhull, Michael","Cakici, Baki","Blønd, Marie","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","Zell, Sonja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384849"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","title":"ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["GDPR","data non use","dramaturgical methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"upon not opening the black box","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85090243080","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384849","identifier":[{"id":"85090243080","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"keyword":["GDPR","data non-use","dramaturgical methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-276723FEC91BFD0CA307B03D82CF6C55"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Cæcilie Sloth","key":"laursenpau","last":"Laursen","locid":"3ef9211c-34b2-4061-ba59-c5800d6a5b32","name":"Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","orcid":"0000-0001-7102-7268","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonja","key":"zellpau","last":"Zell","name":"Zell, Sonja","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3334480.3381813","eisbn":["9781450368193/20/04"],"pages":"1-9","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI ’20 Extended Abstracts"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090243080"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Upon Not Opening The Black Box","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384849","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Pull requests are a method to facilitate review and management of contribution in distributed software development. Software developers author commits, and present them in a pull request to be inspected by maintainers and reviewers. The success and sustainability of communities depends on ongoing contributions, but rejections decrease motivation of contributors. We carried out a a qualitative study to understand the mechanisms of evaluating PRs in open source software (FOSS) communities from developers and maintainers perspective. We interviewed 30 participants from five different FOSS communities. The data shows that acceptance of contributions depends not only on technical criteria, but also significantly on social and strategic aspects. This paper identifies three PR governance styles found in the studied communities: (1) protective, (2) equitable and (3) lenient. Each one of these styles has its particularities. While the protective style values trustworthiness and reliability of the contributor, the lenient style believes in creating a positive and welcoming environment where contributors are mentored to evolve contributions until they meet the community standards. Despite the differences, these governance styles have a commonality, they all safeguard the quality of the software.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Cohn, Marisa","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383794"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Open source software","Pull Request","Software quality"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"how do foss communities decide to accept pull requests","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85048582/EASE_2020_Final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383794","identifier":[{"id":"85090848681","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-ae2d1ebe-9b1e-4dfd-a5d3-3d0ea3c2ec5a"],"keyword":["Open source software","Pull Request","Software Quality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-21AB99A46227BC4D6F9CE885AEAFEAC8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85048582/EASE_2020_Final.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3383219.3383242","isbn":["9781450377317"],"pages":"220-229","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"EASE '20: Proceedings of the Evaluation and Assessment in Software Engineering"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090848681"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ae2d1ebe-9b1e-4dfd-a5d3-3d0ea3c2ec5a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"How Do FOSS Communities Decide to Accept Pull Requests?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383794","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Open source software communities have demonstrated that they can produce high quality results. The overall success of peer code review, commonly used in open source projects, has likely contributed strongly to this success. Code review is an emotionally loaded practice, with public exposure of reputation and ample opportunities for conflict. We set off to ask why code review works for open source communities, despite this inherent challenge. We interviewed 21 open source contributors from four communities and participated in meetings of ROS community devoted to implementation of the code review process. It appears that the hacker ethic is a key reason behind the success of code review in FOSS communities. It is built around the ethic of passion and the ethic of caring. Furthermore, we observed that tasks of code review are performed with strong intrinsic motivation, supported by many non-material extrinsic motivation mechanisms, such as desire to learn, to grow reputation, or to improve one’s positioning on the job market. In the paper, we describe the study design, analyze the collected data and formulate 20 proposals for how what we know about hacker ethics and human and social aspects of code review, could be exploited to improve the effectiveness of the practice in software projects.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Alami, Adam","Cohn, Marisa","Wasowski, Andrzej"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384635"],"event":{"acronym":"ICSE","title":"International Conference on Software Engineering"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Open Source Code Review Motivation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"why does code review work for open source software communities","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83823851/Why_Does_Code_Review_Work_for_Open_Source_Software_Communities_CRC.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384635","identifier":[{"id":"85072274434","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-e4c4deef-51f8-4683-834f-fa57c5f1aa43"],"keyword":["Open Source, Code Review, Motivation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D4AAAA2B298402D01E66B2273238A69C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83823851/Why_Does_Code_Review_Work_for_Open_Source_Software_Communities_CRC.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Adam","key":"alamipau","last":"Alami","name":"Alami, Adam","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andrzej","key":"wasowskipau","last":"Wasowski","locid":"a39b94ff-e1b6-4b96-8404-486167a9123d","name":"Wasowski, Andrzej","orcid":"0000-0003-0532-2685","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/icse.2019.00111","publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"2019 IEEE/ACM 41st International Conference on Software Engineering (ICSE)"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072274434"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-e4c4deef-51f8-4683-834f-fa57c5f1aa43"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Why Does Code Review Work for Open Source Software Communities?","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384635","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This chapter considers how STS might study software as a timely object—that is, an object subject to continuous change and lived with over time as it evolves. As software engineers well know, code is lively and changing. Designs are criticized for being “frozen” if they cannot adapt and evolve alongside code as it changes. Yet at the same time, code persists. Legacy systems, while given little attention in social analyses of software, are the majority of the software that we rely upon. Much STS literature on software has emphasized code’s promiscuous evolvability, how its mutability and dynamism lead it to its inscrutability. However, this difficulty to pin down code sufficiently in order to represent knowledge about it belongs not only to the STS analyst but also to engineers who work closely with code, particularly the software maintainers who deal with software as a continuously evolving object over the long dureé. While STS has done much to unmask the presumed neutrality, immateriality, and automaticity of software, revealing instead the highly imbricated social relationality of software-in-the-making, less attention has been placed on how coders working to maintain legacy systems grapple with this relationality as part and parcel of the nature of working with code. It is this sensibility towards temporality that I argue should guide STS approaches to software, its lifecycles of change, its lifetimes within organizational practice, and the lived experiences of its dynamism over time. This chapter argues that the significance of software’s materiality for the politics of computational work lies not only in its present performances—however hard these may be to trace—but also in its forms of duration, the entangled lifetimes of careers, professional identities, and programming languages and paradigms, all of which come and go. This of course still requires an STS understanding of code as a relational assemblage but also emphasizes that these entanglements have duration—that the relationality of code exists not only in the evocative present of runtime but also in the shared histories and anticipated futures that a body of code binds together.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381854"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digital STS","Lifetimes","Representation","STS","Software Studies","algorithmic media"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"keeping software present software as a timely object for digital sts","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381854","ids":["itu-26734431-5cf8-4d93-9cac-7b211cf1d101"],"keyword":["Digital STS","Lifetimes","Representation","STS","Software Studies","algorithmic media"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-CA6C42C256E3010D0500BC374C353B09"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Janet","key":"vertesipedh","last":"Vertesi","name":"Vertesi, Janet","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"David","key":"ribespedh","last":"Ribes","name":"Ribes, David","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9780691190600"],"isbn":["9780691187075","9780691187082"],"pages":"423-446","publisher":"Princeton University Press","status":"p","title":"Digital STS: A Field Guide for Science &amp; Technology Studies"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-26734431-5cf8-4d93-9cac-7b211cf1d101"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Keeping Software Present: Software as a Timely Object for Digital STS","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381854","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"This article puts forward a bestiary of digital monsters. By bringing into dialogue scholarship in monster theory with that in science and technology studies, we develop the idea of the bestiary as a way of exploring sites where digital monsters are made. We discuss the role of bestiaries in narrating anxieties about the present. We proceed to populate our bestiary with various sociotechnical ‘beasts’ arising in collaborative research project on new data relations in Denmark. The paper argues for the place of the ever-incomplete bestiary in understanding digital monsters, for the bestiary’s role as a gathering point within our project, and for its capacities to speak beyond a single research setting. Through the bestiary, we look toward the ways we already live with monsters and to the forms of analysis available for describing the beasts in our midst.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Burnett, John Mark","Cohn, Marisa","Gad, Christopher","Hockenhull, Michael","Jørgensen, Bastian","Maguire, James","Ojala, Mace","Winthereik, Brit Ross"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382266"],"event":{"acronym":"IFIP WG82","title":"IFIP WG 8.2 Working Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digitization","Ethnography","Monsters"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a bestiary of digital monsters","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83634356/A_Bestiary_of_Digital_Monsters_Final.docm","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382266","identifier":[{"id":"85058283529","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-434ca7be-fac7-4c44-a1d2-399ce958a1d1"],"keyword":["Digitization","ethnography","monsters"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1D42BA173AC8A6F50CDC7BF820FD5E9B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83634356/A_Bestiary_of_Digital_Monsters_Final.docm","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"John Mark","key":"burnettpau","last":"Burnett","name":"Burnett, John Mark","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bastian","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Bastian","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"maguirepau",